{
  "id": "server.health",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/api/health",
  "summary": "What the server is bound to, how its worker and queue are doing, and where it stands on the card. Never refuses: a server that answers at all can say what is wrong with it. The worker is the bound folder's: with nothing bound there is none, so `alive` is false and says nothing is wrong. `queue` counts the jobs `queued`, `running` - every one: jobs on different models answer at the same time, each model one at a time - and `held` - each held for a person's check once its request was built (submitted with `check: true`): it waits on a person, not on the card, and nothing runs for it until it is sent or cancelled (`server.jobs.held`, `server.jobs.send`). `queue.last` is what happened to the last job that RAN - the honest evidence that generation works. `queue.scheduled` (S18) is there while scheduled work waits: how many batch jobs are `planned` for a time still to come and the soonest of those times (`next_at`, seconds since the epoch), and how many wait `for_room` on the card, with the first one's words (`room`) - the numbers, what programs outside the bootstrap hold, and that it starts by itself once it fits; each is `queued`, and none holds the card meanwhile. The two fault records say whether the job store could not be read and whether a dead worker could not be restarted; `count` 0 means never. `card.coordinator` is `none` when the server keeps the card in-process and no coordinator is configured. `card.holding` is whether this server holds the card: its rental, with a coordinator; without one, its pipeline holding the card - the machine's lock, or the stand-in's lease - with nothing loaded too, and whether or not a job is running. `actor` is this server's own id, minted when it started: the name it holds its folder by, and the start of every generation's handle. `contract_version` is the version of the contract this server serves - a short hash of its routes, shapes, id grammar and validators (`contract_version()` in the contract's validate.py) - so a client can tell whether it speaks the same contract. `runner` is the runner's own answer when the runner is behind the queue - of the models up side by side, the one used last and its load id; a load under way (one at a time); how many generations hold a model, and which models they hold (`in_use_models`, by name: answers to different models run at once); the lease on the card and who holds it - and null when there is no runner (no backend, or the echo). `pipeline` is the card held from a pipeline's open to its close, and where it stands: `closed`, `waiting` for the card, `holding` it - with nothing loaded too - `degraded` (held, but the card is over-full; the coordinator's word) or `lost` (the card was taken back: its models came off, and an application's pipeline has asked for a new ticket by itself), with `why`, `since` when, its `label`, the `models` it keeps (pinned), and `up`: every model on the card and answering its health check, side by side - the ones it keeps and the ones loaded on demand for jobs. `opened_by` says whose it is: the application's, the one the server opened as it started, or the queue's own for its work. Closed, it says why it closed; never opened, every field but `state` and `models` is null. Null when there is no runner.",
  "request": null,
